The normalized Z-score is another metric to assess marker gene specificity, alongside the log2FC. This is computed by first calculating the mean and standard deviation of the gene’s expression across all nontarget cell types. The formulas for the Z-score and normalized Z-score are:
\begin{array}{c} \text {Z - score} = \frac {\exp_ {\text {target}} + \exp_ {\text {others}}}{\sigma_ {\text {others}} + \epsilon}, \\ \text {Normalized Z - score} = \left\{ \begin{array}{l l} 0, & \text {Z - score} \leq 0 \\ \log_ {1 0} \left(\text {Z - score} + 1\right), & \text {Z - score} > 0 \end{array} \right. \end{array}
where $\mathrm { e x p } _ { \mathrm { t a r g e t } }$ and $\mathsf { e x p } _ { \mathsf { o t h e r s } }$ are the mean expressions in the target and non-target cell types, and $\sigma _ { 0 } \mathrm { { t h e r s } } ^ { \mathrm { { i s } } }$ the standard deviation across non-target types. To ensure numerical stability, we add a small epsilon value.
